-- allium: 3
-- 21-model-risk-management.allium

-- Scope: Governance of the models that price and aggregate: which models
--   exist, who owns them, who has validated them independently, how they are
--   monitored once in production, and what stops a model that has not been
--   validated from reaching production (D-050).
-- Includes: the model inventory with risk tiers, independent validation
--   records and their expiry, conditions attached to a validation, scheduled
--   benchmark runs against the independent reference, breaches and their
--   review, the gate on production releases, the rule against overrides.
-- Excludes:
--   - How a validator validates: the bank's validation function does the
--     work; Veridian records outcome, scope, conditions and evidence.
--   - The models' own content and golden cross-checks (09, 17, 18): reused.
--   - FRTB-SA back-testing: the standardised approach has none, so none is
--     specified.
--   - Overlays or manual adjustments of results: not allowed (see below).
-- Dependencies: 01, 02, 05, 13.
--
-- Principles (D-050):
--   - Models, owners and tiers are configuration, registered when a release
--     activates, like schedules and perimeters. Validations and benchmark
--     results are records the platform keeps at runtime, because they happen
--     after the code is written.
--   - A model version is an exact code hash (02). Validation attaches to a
--     version, so a changed model is not covered by the old validation.
--   - Independence is enforced, not assumed: the validator is neither the
--     model's owner nor the author of the release that carried it.
--   - There are no overrides. A figure is always reproducible from inputs
--     (02, 19); a model's known limitations are recorded and enforced as
--     restrictions, and any adjustment belongs to the bank's own downstream
--     process outside Veridian.

-- Split (D-070): this file holds the inventory and the actors;
--   21b-model-validation validations and their lifecycle;
--   21c-benchmarks-and-breaches benchmark runs and breaches.

use "./01-bitemporal-data.allium" as data
use "./02-calculation-graph.allium" as graph
use "./05-configuration-release.allium" as cfg
use "./13-report-review-alerts-trends.allium" as review
use "./21b-model-validation.allium" as val
use "./21c-benchmarks-and-breaches.allium" as brc

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

contract ModelGovernance {
    @invariant ScopeEnforced
        -- A validation states the scope the model is approved for: instrument
        -- kinds, currencies, tenors and moneyness ranges, data classes. The
        -- planner rejects a position outside it with a deterministic
        -- rejection naming the model and the limit (02, ComputationRejected);
        -- it never prices outside the approved scope silently.

    @invariant NoOverrides
        -- No user can replace, adjust or add to a calculated result. There is
        -- no overlay entity and no field a result can be edited in; a
        -- different answer is a different model version or different inputs.

    @invariant ConditionsAreTracked
        -- Conditions on an approval are either restrictions of the scope
        -- (enforced as above) or remediation with a due date, which raises an
        -- alert to the model's owner when overdue.

    @invariant ValidationFollowsCode
        -- A validation attaches to one model version (one code hash). A new
        -- version is validated afresh; a release that merely carries an
        -- unchanged version forward keeps its validation (02).

    @invariant IndependentReference
        -- Benchmark runs compare the model with the independent reference
        -- (the QuantLib vectors and runs of D-010 and D-049), never with the
        -- same code under another name.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- high: models that drive regulatory capital or whose failure is hard to
-- see (lattice, calibration, FRTB aggregation). medium and low: simpler
-- models. The tier sets how often a model is validated and benchmarked.
enum ModelTier { high | medium | low }

enum ValidationOutcome { approved | approved_with_conditions | rejected }

-- A model in the inventory: defined in a configuration release.
entity ModelInventoryEntry {
    function_name: String
    owner: data/User
    tier: ModelTier
    purpose: String
    definition_hash: String
    release: cfg/ConfigurationRelease
    next_benchmark_due: Timestamp?
    status: in_inventory | retired

    transitions status {
        in_inventory -> retired
        terminal: retired
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Defaults are placeholders for the bank's own model risk policy.
config {
    validation_interval_high: Duration = 365.days
    validation_interval_medium: Duration = 730.days
    validation_interval_low: Duration = 1095.days
    benchmark_interval_high: Duration = 7.days
    benchmark_interval_medium: Duration = 30.days
    benchmark_interval_low: Duration = 90.days
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The inventory comes only from configuration releases.
rule RegisterReleaseInventory {
    when: cfg/ReleaseActivated(release, previous)
    ensures:
        for d in release_inventory(release):
            InventoryEntryDefined(release: release, definition: d)
        for e in inventory_dropped_by(release):
            InventoryEntryDropped(entry: e)
}

rule CreateInventoryEntry {
    when: InventoryEntryDefined(release, definition)
    requires: not exists ModelInventoryEntry{definition_hash: inventory_definition_hash(definition)}
    ensures: ModelInventoryEntry.created(
        function_name: inventory_function_name(definition),
        owner: inventory_owner(definition),
        tier: inventory_tier(definition),
        purpose: inventory_purpose(definition),
        definition_hash: inventory_definition_hash(definition),
        release: release,
        next_benchmark_due: null,
        status: in_inventory
    )
}

rule RetireInventoryEntry {
    when: InventoryEntryDropped(entry)
    requires: entry.status = in_inventory
    ensures: entry.status = retired
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

actor ModelValidator {
    identified_by: data/User
}

actor ModelOwner {
    identified_by: data/User
}

actor BenchmarkWorker {
    identified_by: data/User
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface ModelInventoryView {
    facing owner: ModelOwner
    context entry: ModelInventoryEntry where owner = owner

    exposes:
        entry.function_name
        entry.tier
        entry.purpose
        entry.next_benchmark_due
        entry.status
        current_validation_of(entry)
        open_breaches_of(entry)

    contracts:
        demands ModelGovernance
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Deferred Specifications
------------------------------------------------------------

-- release_inventory(release), inventory_* accessors, inventory_dropped_by:
--   from the release artefacts (05): the inventory entries a release defines,
--   and the entries the previous release had that this one no longer lists.
-- validation_interval(tier), benchmark_interval(tier): the configuration
--   values above by tier.
-- other_current_validations(model_version, keep): the other validations in
--   state current for that version.
-- current_validation_of, open_breaches_of: queries.
-- BenchmarkRequested: event for the benchmark worker (a graph request of the
--   benchmark job), by tier.
-- models_cleared(release): used by spec 05's gate, true when every model
--   version (the graph model version, D-060) the release adds or changes,
--   compared with the active release of
--   that kind, has a current validation with outcome approved or
--   approved_with_conditions whose scope covers the release's use, and its
--   inventory entry has no open breach. Versions carried forward unchanged are
--   not looked at, so a release that changes no model always passes (D-058).

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Resolved (D-050): inventory with tiers; validation by someone independent
-- of owner and release author; scheduled benchmarks against the independent
-- reference with a gate on production releases; no overrides.
open question "The bank's own tiering criteria, validation and benchmark intervals (the defaults are placeholders): confirm against its model risk policy (e.g. SR 11-7, PRA SS1/23, ECB guide)."
-- Resolved (D-054): a withdrawn validation or an unresolved breach blocks new
-- releases only; the model in the active release keeps running, the finding
-- is flagged on every run that uses the model and escalated, and a new
-- release is the way to replace it.
open question "A validation that is overdue for long: should the model be suspended after a grace period?"
open question "Who is the bank's validation function and is it separate from the quant team that owns the models?"
open question "Retention of validation records and benchmark results: same 10 years as reported figures (13), to be confirmed with compliance."
-- Resolved (D-058): the release gate applies to the model versions a release adds or changes, compared with the active release; unchanged versions are not looked at, so one lapsed validation no longer blocks a release that changes no model (found by the release-gate model, models/README.md).
-- Resolved (D-060): a model version is the graph model version (spec 02), the one that enters calc_id. A version is new or changed when the graph model version in the release differs from the one in the active release of that kind. The inventory entry records the graph model version a validation was made against, so validation follows the executing code. A release that removes a model needs no validation but names the removal.
open question "A graph model version bump that changes no behaviour (refactoring) needs a fresh validation under D-060. Is a lighter path wanted, for example a validator confirming equivalence on the golden vectors, or is a full revalidation accepted?"
